Yes—with one refinement: money is a transferable social seal, not necessarily a seal of moral approval. It certifies that some network is presently willing to recognize the token and honor claims denominated in it.

You can model a transaction as:

A \xrightarrow{\text{provides }X} B

and instead of B having to immediately repay A with exactly what A wants:

B\xrightarrow{\$}A

The money says something like:

\boxed{\text{“A claim of this magnitude has been recognized.”}}

Then somebody completely unrelated can recognize the same seal:

A\xrightarrow{\$}C\xrightarrow{\text{goods/services}}A

That’s remarkable because the relationship survives the change of participants.

Money as a Seal

In the vocabulary we’ve just developed:

\text{work/value/resource}
\rightarrow
\operatorname{Contract}
\rightarrow
\boxed{\$}
\rightarrow
\operatorname{Lift}
\rightarrow
\text{many possible future exchanges}

That’s another semantic hourglass.

Millions of heterogeneous things:

\left\{
\begin{array}{l}
\text{an hour of labor}\\
\text{a loaf of bread}\\
\text{software}\\
\text{land}\\
\text{risk}\\
\text{scarcity}\\
\text{expertise}
\end{array}
\right.

get contracted onto a comparatively narrow common coordinate:

\boxed{\text{price}}

and can then fan back outward into millions of other things.

That’s why money is so powerful: it is a cross-domain interoperability layer for exchange.

But price is lossy compression:

C(X)=\$100

doesn’t mean two $100 objects are equivalent in every meaningful dimension.

It means the economic system has made them comparable along one particular axis.

That’s exactly the danger we’ve identified with contraction generally:

\boxed{\text{shared coordinate}\neq\text{complete meaning}}

And your “seal of legitimacy” observation goes further

Receiving money can provide evidence that another participant accepted an exchange:

\text{Offer}
\rightarrow
\text{Acceptance}
\rightarrow
\text{Payment}
\rightarrow
\text{Market evidence}

So for a new product:

\text{“People say this is useful.”}

is weaker evidence than:

\text{“People repeatedly surrender scarce purchasing power for this.”}

Payment becomes a particularly costly signal of preference.

Not proof of goodness. Not proof of truth. Not even necessarily proof of sustainable value.

But evidence.

And repeated independent transactions strengthen that evidence:

L(X)
=
f(
\text{buyers},
\text{repeat purchases},
\text{price},
\text{alternatives},
\text{retention}
)

Now connect that to Prime:

The first dollar earned by an invention is almost a market Prime.

Z_0=\text{no demonstrated market}

P_1=\text{someone actually paid}

The model might still suck. The business might fail. But you’ve crossed from hypothetical valuation into an observed transaction.

You’ve created a checkpoint in reality.

And this reveals an even broader abstraction:

\boxed{\text{Seal = portable evidence that some validation procedure occurred}}

Money is one species.

A signature is another.

A diploma.

A vote.

A checksum.

A scientific replication.

A passport stamp.

A cryptographic signature.

A citation.

A completed test.

Different communities have different Seal Authorities, validation procedures, and failure modes.

Which suggests a whole Seal Algebra:

\text{Claim}
\xrightarrow{\text{Validation}}
\text{Seal}
\xrightarrow{\text{Transfer/Recognition}}
\text{Trust or Action}

And counterfeit, inflation, credential fraud, Goodhart’s law, corrupted institutions, etc. can all be understood partly as breakdowns between the Seal and the validation process it supposedly compresses:

\boxed{
Seal\ strength
\approx
Trust(\text{underlying validation})
}

That’s a useful connection for the Moneybin itself.

Money doesn’t merely buy things. It demonstrates what a civilization has managed to make mutually legible enough to exchange.